# Design: Flatten auth chain for video-scoped guards

## Problem
requireProjectAccess, requireVideoAccess, and
requireVideoManagementAccess each delegated to requireTeamAccess,
which called requireUser again. Every project- and video-scoped
Convex call paid a second getUserIdentity round-trip plus the
same teamMembers lookup that the caller could have done itself.

## Approach
Ported the guard-flattening technique from upstream lawn commit
62c0463 onto the fork guards. Each guard now does one
getUserIdentity read, its own entity gets, and one direct
teamMembers by_team_and_user .unique() lookup via the existing
getTeamMembership helper. The membership and role decisions moved
into shared module-private helpers
(requireTeamMembershipOrThrow, requireRoleOrThrow) so the logic
stays single-sourced across the four guards.

## Key Trade-offs
- Authorization is NOT flattened: check order is preserved
  exactly (membership denial, then video grant denial via
  canTeamMemberAccessVideo, then role denial in requireVideoAccess)
  so denial codes and messages stay byte-identical for clients.
- Slight duplication of the lookup wiring across guards is
  accepted, mirroring upstream, to remove the extra round-trip.
- requireVideoMemberAccess in convex/videoActions.ts keeps its
  ctx.runQuery(api.videos.get) shape (actions cannot use ctx.db,
  and adding a query is blocked: no Convex credentials to
  regenerate _generated). It inherits the flattening because
  videos.get calls the flattened requireVideoAccess.

## Verification
convex/auth.test.ts gained guard-level tests over a fake ctx:
role decisions (viewer/member/admin/owner/non-member), grant-based
access on restricted videos, unchanged ConvexError codes and
messages, and assertions that each guard call performs exactly
one getUserIdentity read and one teamMembers lookup.

